<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Faridkot State</h1><p class="st-meta">1763 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Faridkot</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sikh princely state of the Malwa region of Punjab, ruled by Brar Jat Rajas from 1763 until 1948.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamir-singh-of-faridkot">Hamir Singh</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brar-clan">Brar Jat</a> chief, established himself at Faridkot around 1763, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> power spread across the plains after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrani-empire">Durrani</a> wars. The town took its name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-farid">Baba Farid</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Faridkot came under British protection in 1809. Raja <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pahar-singh-of-faridkot">Pahar Singh</a> sided with the British in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-sikh-war">First Anglo-Sikh War</a> in 1845 and was rewarded with land. The state had an 11-gun salute and a mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> rural population; its rulers built schools, a hospital and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a> Mahal palace.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harinder-singh-brar">Harinder Singh Brar</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947 and Faridkot was merged into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patiala-and-east-punjab-states-union">Patiala and East Punjab States Union</a> in 1948. His death in 1989 was followed by a long legal dispute over the family estate.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/faridkot-state.jpg" alt="Faridkot State" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Faridkot_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1763 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Faridkot</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hamir Singh, Pahar Singh, Harinder Singh Brar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sikhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Punjabi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Faridkot</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=faridkot-state" aria-label="Faridkot State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6616 1059 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Faridkot State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6936 1315L7364 1601M6936 1315L7364 1602M6936 1315L7003 1288"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7003 1288h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6936 1315h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6936 1315h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Faridkot. <a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=faridkot-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
